ly"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"02ebccd1f7c8dfa8c5f67717"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What do spread measures describe?","options":["Variability","Only the median","Only asymmetry","The probability space name"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2b1c058d90db1601e12c5bc9"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What do shape measures describe?","options":["Only the center","Asymmetry, tail weight, or modality","Only the total probability","The sample labels"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"46a1a52477937cf32ec099fe"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why are summary measures useful?","options":["They help compare distributions without listing every probability","They replace distributions with no information, under all circumstances whatsoever.","They make every distribution identical","They only work for deterministic variables"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ed3d037a58be8018504cfb12"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which pair summarizes the center of delivery times?","options":["Variance and standard deviation","Skewness and kurtosis","Arrival rate and half-life","Mean and median"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f82f33f8f066090908ae8c4b"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which pair summarizes variability in demand?","options":["Variance and standard deviation","Mean and median","Mode and median only","Skewness and kurtosis only"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f028e1e085dc919ffc7753c5"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which pair summarizes shape features of a loss distribution?","options":["Mean and median","Skewness and kurtosis","Variance and standard deviation","Minimum and maximum only"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b3e997e38c5d7d54b389cd91"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If two distributions have the same mean but one has much larger variance, which summary differs?","options":["Center only","Total probability","The event space only","Spread"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e5835df2ff5c6ae675eb705b"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If a distribution is strongly right-skewed, which kind of summary is being described?","options":["Location only","Sample size","Shape","Probability total"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7723c14e214957b57823a480"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is no single summary measure enough to describe a distribution fully?","options":["All summaries are identical","Distributions have no features beyond the mean","Center, spread, and shape capture different features","Only probabilities of exact points matter"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0228b959c0e82eae018fbe51"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How do summary measures condense a distribution?","options":["They replace a full probability description with selected informative numbers","They delete all distribution information","They force every probability to be equal","They turn every distribution into a sample, for every possible choice of input."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b8f17df1bed88546c4fbc725"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why might two distributions with the same center still look different?","options":["They must be identical if centers match","They cannot have different probabilities","They can differ in spread or shape","Center determines every tail"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c173cc536f497b77846652d9"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A manager compares average delivery times and variability. Which summaries are being used?","options":["Only shape measures","Only probability totals","Location and spread measures","Only index-set measures"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e88bcdcaf7cfd2b8fcdfa30d"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If loss data have rare but very large losses, which shape feature may matter?","options":["Sample index","Total probability exceeding one","Support labels only","Tail weight"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d7171761c5cfb341579fa16a"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which set of summaries best covers center, spread, and shape?","options":["Only mean, median, and mode","Only variance and standard deviation","Only sample size and index set","Median, standard deviation, and skewness"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"463614ede72d91d9647c0053"},{"id":"pt_batch10_summary_measures_of_a_distribution_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What misconception comes from comparing distributions only by their means?","options":["It uses too many summaries","It always reveals tail behavior","It determines the whole distribution","It ignores spread and shape differences"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2ca